<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Islamic Courts Union</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2006">2006</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Islamic Courts Union was an alliance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">sharia</a> courts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a> that took control of Mogadishu in June 2006 after defeating US-backed warlords, bringing a brief period of order to the capital. Its expansion and the rise of hard-line factions provoked an Ethiopian invasion in December 2006 that drove it from power. Its militant youth wing, al-Shabaab, then launched a long insurgency.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/islamic-courts-union.jpg" alt="Islamic Courts Union" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Guardafuuii · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ICU_Somalia_Emblem.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> state collapsed in January 1991, well-known scholars such as Sheikh Sharif Sharafow, Sheikh Ibrahim Suley and Sheikh Mohamed Moallin Hassan set up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">Sharia</a> courts in Mogadishu to protect civilians hunted by clan militias and to deal with lawlessness. The first courts worked at neighbourhood level, mostly on petty crime and family disputes, and took no side in clan or national politics. More courts opened in northern Mogadishu in 1994 and 1995 and used security forces to guard schools and hospitals. In April 1999 several courts united to seize the Bakaara Market from warlords, and in 2000 eleven courts formed the Islamic Courts Council with a single militia. In 2001 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transitional-national-government">Transitional National Government</a> absorbed the courts and their militia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From 2003 the American Central Intelligence Agency conducted covert operations against the courts, funding Mogadishu warlords who formed the Alliance for the Restoration of Peace and Counter-Terrorism. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> feared that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a> would become a haven for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qaeda">Al-Qaeda</a>. After a wave of assassinations and kidnappings from 2005, in which the courts blamed US operations and warlords, a force known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-shabaab">Al-Shabaab</a>, 'the Youth', emerged, related to but seemingly autonomous of the courts. On 5 June 2006 the Islamic Courts Union defeated the warlord alliance in the Second Battle of Mogadishu and became the first group since 1991 to control the whole city.</p><p>The airport and seaport reopened, and the courts revived police stations and banned open possession of unregistered firearms. They also banned khat and cigarettes, and some of these social policies were unpopular. Hassan Dahir Aweys of the Shura Council, who rejected comparisons with the Taliban, led a more hardline wing, while Sheikh Sharif Ahmed led a more moderate one. The first clash with Ethiopian forces came at Burhakaba on 9 October 2006. In late December 2006 about 50,000 Ethiopian troops invaded, and Ethiopian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transitional-federal-government">Transitional Federal Government</a> troops entered Mogadishu in the last days of the year, with US support. Scholars such as Ted Dagne and William R. Polk have said the courts had not provoked the invasion.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After the invasion much of the courts' structure disintegrated. About two-thirds of Mogadishu's residents fled between 2007 and 2008. Leaders who had sought refuge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a> founded the Alliance for the Re-liberation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a> in September 2007, and its peace accord with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transitional-federal-government">Transitional Federal Government</a> was signed in June 2008. By the end of 2008 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-shabaab">Al-Shabaab</a> had become one of the dominant insurgent groups, eclipsing the courts. Former chairman Sharif Ahmed became president of Somalia in 2009.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Islamic Courts Union” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 2006 the <b>Islamic Courts Union</b> took Mogadishu, provoking Ethiopian intervention, from which the militant al-Shabaab emerged.</blockquote><a href="/state/somalia">Somalia</a></li></ul></section>
